+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+The category service currently gathers the selected lineage, removes category links from products, and deletes products that have no remaining category. Category deletion also relies on entity cascades for descendants. Product-category links are many-to-many, while carts and orders retain product references, so product deletion is the source of the commerce regressions.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- Make deletion transactional and limited to category rows, descendants, and product-category join rows.
+- Preserve product entities and all product-owned data regardless of remaining category count.
+- Ensure missing category ids are surfaced as resource-not-found responses.
+- Add API/service regression tests covering descendant and commerce behavior.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- Changing product visibility or search semantics beyond removal of deleted category associations.
+- Migrating existing orphaned products created by older buggy deletions.
+- Changing cart or order schemas.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- Resolve and validate the category for the merchant before invoking deletion. A missing or cross-store id must fail with the existing not-found/authorization behavior rather than entering deletion logic.
+- Build the complete category tree from the selected category lineage, then remove each category from the managed product association set and save the product. Never call product deletion based on category count; products are independent catalog records.
+- Delete descendants before the root (or use the existing cascade only after associations have been detached) so foreign-key constraints and the product-category join table cannot retain references.
+- Keep the change in the category service transaction boundary so category rows and association updates succeed or roll back together.
+- Test at the service level for persistence and at the API level for HTTP 404 and product/category visibility; use existing cart/order fixtures to verify retained product references.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Risk] Large category trees can require many product updates. → Use one deduplicated product set and update each product once per deletion transaction.
+- [Risk] Lazy associations may be stale in the persistence context. → Reload products/categories through service or repository methods before modifying associations and verify after transaction completion.
+- [Risk] Existing cascade mappings may delete descendants automatically. → Explicitly detach product links first and delete in a deterministic order, with integration tests for nested trees.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+Deploy the service/API change and regression tests. No data migration is required; rollback is a normal application rollback because the change only affects future deletion transactions.
